response:
 In Rust, you can define the `is_even` function using a loop instead of multiple `if-else` statements for better readability and code duplication avoidance. Here's how you can transform the given JavaScript code into Rust:

```rust
fn is_even(n: i32) -> bool {
 match n {
     0 => true,
     1 | 3 | 5 | 7 | 9 => false,
     _ => {
         for i in 2..=n / 2 {
             if n % i == 0 {
                 return true;
             }
         }
         false
     }
 }
}
```

This Rust version uses pattern matching with the `match` keyword to handle some edge cases directly. The remaining numbers are handled using an `_` wildcard pattern that triggers the loop part. This loop checks divisibility by every number up to half of the input number `n`. If a factor is found, it returns `true` as the number is even. Otherwise, it returns `false`.

