 To write R code that checks if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric, you can use the `sapply()` function with the `as.numeric()` and `is.na()` functions. Here's an example:

```R
# Sample character vector
char_vec <- c("1", "2.5", "three", NA, "5", "six")

# Function to check if each element can be converted to numeric
can_convert <- function(x) {
  if (is.na(x)) return(FALSE)
  if (is.numeric(as.numeric(x))) return(TRUE)
  else return(FALSE)
}

# Apply function to each element of character vector
numeric_elements <- sapply(char_vec, can_convert)

# Check if all elements are numeric
if (all(numeric_elements)) {
  print("All non-NA elements in the character vector can be converted to numeric.")
} else {
  print("Not all non-NA elements in the character vector can be converted to numeric.")
}
```

This code defines a helper function called `can_convert()` which takes one argument, an element from the character vector. It returns `TRUE` if the element is not NA and can be converted to numeric using `as.numeric()`, otherwise it returns `FALSE`. The `sapply()` function applies this helper function to each element of the character vector, and `all()` checks if all returned values are `TRUE`.

