 Here's a simple decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` environment variable and calls the next middleware function if it equals "true", otherwise returns `false`. This decorator prioritizes helpfulness by providing clear error messages and explanations, as well as some level of verbosity to help understand what's happening in the code.

```typescript
import { createParamDecorator, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

export const CheckNodeEnv = () => {
  return (target: Function, key: string, descriptor: PropertyDescriptor) => {
    const originalMethod = descriptor.value;

    descriptor.value = async function (...args: any[]) {
      if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'true') {
        // Call the next middleware function if NODE_ENV is set to 'true'
        await descriptor.next(args);
      } else {
        throw new Error('NODE_ENV is not set to "true". Please make sure your application is running in development mode.');
      }
    };

    Reflect.defineMetadata('decoratorName', 'checkNodeEnv', target, key);
    return descriptor;
  };
};

// Usage example:
@CheckNodeEnv()
async myMiddlewareFunction(@Req() req, @Res() res, next: Function): Promise<void> {
  // Your middleware logic here
}
```

To use this decorator, simply apply the `@CheckNodeEnv()` decorator on the middleware function. The middleware will only be executed when `NODE_ENV` is set to "true". If `NODE_ENV` is not set or has another value, an error will be thrown with a clear message explaining the issue.

