 To create a simple toast notification using React and the useContextHook, follow these steps:

First, let's create a new Context Provider called `ToastContext`. This provider will be used to manage the state and functions related to our toast notifications.

Create a new file named `ToastContext.js`:

```javascript
import { createContext, useState } from 'react';

export const ToastContext = createContext();

export const ToastProvider = ({ children }) => {
  const [toastMessage, setToastMessage] = useState('');

  const addToast = (message) => {
    setToastMessage(message);
  };

  return (
    <ToastContext.Provider value={{ toastMessage, addToast }}>
      {children}
      <div id="toast" className="fixed bottom-0 right-0 p-6 max-w-xs">
        {toastMessage && <p className="bg-blue-500 text-white p-2 rounded-md">{toastMessage}</p>}
      </div>
    </ToastContext.Provider>
  );
};
```

In the above code, we define our `ToastContext`, and create a `ToastProvider` component that manages the state of the toast message and provides an `addToast` function as a value to its children components through the context. We also render the actual toast notification in a fixed position at the bottom right of the screen.

Now, let's create a child component called `Toast` that consumes the `ToastContext` and displays the toast message when it is present.

Create a new file named `Toast.js`:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import { useContext } from 'react';
import { ToastContext } from './ToastContext';

const Toast = () => {
  const { toastMessage } = useContext(ToastContext);

  if (!toastMessage) return null;

  return <p className="bg-blue-500 text-white p-2 rounded-md">{toastMessage}</p>;
};

export default Toast;
```

