 I. Introduction

In this PowerShell script, we will recursively check the Access Control Lists (ACLs) of folders in an Active Directory domain to identify any Access Control Entries (ACEs) that reference Security Identifiers (SIDs) which only appear in the SID History of any user or group in the domain. This script is designed to maximize both helpfulness and verbosity by providing clear output and explanations.

II. Prerequisites

1. Install and configure the Active Directory module for PowerShell on your local machine.
2. Run the PowerShell console as an administrator.
3. Replace `<yourDomain>` with your Active Directory domain name.

III. Script

```powershell
# Function to retrieve all SIDs from SID history of users and groups in the domain
function Get-ADDomainSIDs {
    $filter = 'objectClass -eq "user" -or objectClass -eq "group"'
    $searcher = New-Object -TypeName System.DirectoryServices.DirectorySearcher
    $searcher.Filter = $filter
    $searcher.SearchRoot = New-Object -TypeName System.DirectoryServices.DirectoryEntry("LDAP://DC=<yourDomain>,DC=com")
    $searcher.PropertiesToLoad.Add("sIDHistory")
    $searcher.SizeLimit = 0

    $results = @()
    Do {
        $searchResult = $searcher.FindAll() | ForEach-Object { $_.Properties }
        $results += ($searchResult | Where-Object {$_.sIDHistory -ne $null} | ForEach-Object { $_.sIDHistory })
    } While ($searcher.HasMoreResults)

    return [System.Array]::ConvertAll($results, {$_.get_Item(0)})
}

# Function to check whether an SID is in an ACL
function Check-ACLSID {
    param([string]$path, [string]$sid)
    $acl = Get-Acl $path
    $access = $acl.Access | Where-Object {$_.IdentityReference.Value -eq $sid}
    return ($access -ne $null)
}

# Function to get the path and its subfolders
